  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 221 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nOn Thursday, the main upper low opens up into a trough across the \nnorthern and central Great Plains. However, lower heights retrograde \nsouthwest of the state closing off into a low across southern \nCalifornia by Thursday evening. The initial upper level trough will \nsend a backdoor front through eastern NM during the day Thursday \nresulting in north wind gusts of 40 to 55 mph. Cooler with high \ntemperatures slightly below average behind the backdoor front. A few \nWind Advisories will likely need to be issued across northeast and \neast central NM in subsequent forecast packages. The upper low over \nsouthern California will help pull the backdoor front through the \ngaps of the central mountain chain across central and western NM \nThursday evening into Friday morning. This along with a 1043 mb \nsurface high over eastern Colorado will result in a very strong east \ncanyon wind event for canyon openings across central NM. Easterly \n700 mb winds and cross sections showing some elevated instability \nover Albuquerque show low potential for a mountain overtopping event \nThursday night into early Friday morning. With increasing confidence \nfor wind gusts > 60 mph, a High Wind Watch has been issued for the \nABQ Metro, south central highlands, upper Tularosa Valley and lower \nRio Grande Valley, including Socorro.\n\nGusty east winds will continue across western and central NM during \nthe day on Friday as the surface pressure gradient behind the \nbackdoor front slowly relaxes and easterly 700 mb winds slowly \nweaken. It will be a cool and crisp mid October day areawide with \nhigh temperatures in the mid 50s to low 60s across lower elevations. \nCould have a few showers across far southern areas due to higher \nmoisture pooling across the part of the state. Freeze watches and \nwarnings may be needed for more northern and central highlands and \nvalley locations, excluding the RGV, Friday night into Saturday \nmorning due to temperatures at to slightly below freezing. Cool \ntemperatures on Saturday as the upper low over southern California \nopens up during the afternoon hours. This will result in some \nshowers across western NM late Saturday into early Sunday. Guidance \nhas the trough moving across the state Sunday resulting in a few \nshowers across the western and north central high terrain. Warmer \nwith highs in the 60s to mid 70s across the lower elevations. Even \nmilder Monday as shortwave ridging to upper level southwest flow \nmoves overhead.",
